(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Communication Systems"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Communication Systems"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Communication Systems: Assignments\n\nThree assignments, seven questions each (21 in all), with complete solutions at the end of each assignment. Every number in the solutions was computed with numpy and scipy (the BER values are Monte Carlo simulations with 10^6 to 2 × 10^6 bits per point); reproduce them in Python.\n\n# Assignment 1: Noise, Analog Modulation and PCM (Chapters 1 to 3)\n\n**Q1. Noise.** (a) The thermal noise density at 290 K in W/Hz and dBm/Hz [3] (b) The noise power in 1 MHz and in 20 MHz (dBm) [3] (c) The noise floor of a receiver with a 6 dB noise figure in 20 MHz [4]\n\n**Q2. AM waveform.** The envelope of an AM wave has a maximum of 12 V and a minimum of 4 V. (a) The modulation index and the carrier amplitude [4] (b) The sideband amplitudes [2] (c) The bandwidth for a 5 kHz message [2] (d) The efficiency [2]\n\n**Q3. AM power.** (a) A carrier of 50 W with m = 1: total power and sideband power [3] (b) The same with m = 0.5 [3] (c) A transmitter of total power 1000 W with m = 0.6: the carrier power and sideband power [4]\n\n**Q4. FM.** Find the modulation index and Carson bandwidth for (a) delta_f = 75 kHz, fm = 15 kHz [3] (b) delta_f = 5 kHz, fm = 3 kHz [3] (c) delta_f = 25 kHz, fm = 5 kHz [2] (d) Explain the significance of the Bessel zero at beta = 2.405 [2]\n\n**Q5. AM versus FM.** Compare AM and wideband FM in bandwidth, power efficiency, noise performance, circuit complexity and uses [10]\n\n**Q6. PCM.** (a) The SQNR for 8, 12, 16 and 24 bits [3] (b) The number of bits needed for 40 dB and 60 dB [3] (c) The bit rates of telephone speech (8 kHz, 8 bits) and CD stereo audio (44.1 kHz, 16 bits) [2] (d) The T1 line rate (24 channels plus 8 kb/s framing) [2]\n\n**Q7. Spectrum check.** (a) An AM signal with fc = 100 kHz, fm = 5 kHz and m = 0.8 is analysed with an FFT: what are the spectral lines? [4] (b) An FM signal with fc = 500 kHz, fm = 10 kHz and delta_f = 50 kHz: the amplitudes of the carrier and of the first three sidebands [4] (c) How many sideband pairs contain 98 percent of the power for beta = 5? [2]\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 1\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 1: thermal noise power density N₀ = kT, with k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J/K and T = 290 K.\nStep 2: N₀ = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 290 = **4.0 × 10⁻²¹ W/Hz**.\nStep 3: convert to dBm/Hz: 10 log₁₀(4.0 × 10⁻²¹ W / 1 mW) = 10 log₁₀(4.0 × 10⁻¹⁸) = **-174 dBm/Hz**.\n(b) Step 1: N = kTB = N₀B.\nStep 2 (1 MHz): N = 4.0 × 10⁻²¹ × 10⁶ = 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ W = **-114 dBm** (-174 + 10 log₁₀ 10⁶ = -174 + 60).\nStep 3 (20 MHz): -174 + 10 log₁₀(20 × 10⁶) = -174 + 73 = **-101 dBm**.\n(c) Step 1: noise floor = thermal noise + noise figure.\nStep 2: -174 + 10 log₁₀(20 × 10⁶) + NF = -174 + 73.0 + 6 = **-95.0 dBm**.\n\n**Q2.** Envelope of an AM wave: maximum A_c(1 + m) = 12 V, minimum A_c(1 - m) = 4 V.\n(a) Step 1: add the two: 2A_c = 16, so **A_c = 8 V**.\nStep 2: subtract: 2mA_c = 8, so m = 8/16 = **0.5** (or m = (12 - 4)/(12 + 4)).\n(b) Step 1: each sideband has amplitude mA_c/2 = 0.5 × 8/2 = **2 V**.\n(c) Step 2: bandwidth = 2f_m = 2 × 5 kHz = **10 kHz**.\n(d) Step 3: efficiency η = m²/(2 + m²) = 0.25/2.25 = **11.1 percent** (the share of power in the sidebands).\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Step 1: total power P_t = P_c(1 + m²/2) = 50(1 + 1/2) = 50 × 1.5 = **75 W**.\nStep 2: sideband power = P_t - P_c = 75 - 50 = **25 W**.\n(b) Step 1: m = 0.5: P_t = 50(1 + 0.25/2) = 50 × 1.125 = **56.25 W**.\nStep 2: sideband power = 56.25 - 50 = **6.25 W**.\n(c) Step 1: P_t = 1000 W, m = 0.6: P_c = P_t/(1 + m²/2) = 1000/(1 + 0.18) = 1000/1.18 = **847.5 W**.\nStep 2: sideband power = 1000 - 847.5 = **152.5 W** (15.3 percent of the total).\n\n**Q4.** FM: β = Δf/f_m; Carson bandwidth B = 2(Δf + f_m).\n(a) β = 75/15 = **5**; B = 2(75 + 15) = 2 × 90 = **180 kHz**.\n(b) β = 5/3 = **1.67**; B = 2(5 + 3) = **16 kHz**.\n(c) β = 25/5 = **5**; B = 2(25 + 5) = **60 kHz**.\n(d) The carrier amplitude of a single-tone FM signal is proportional to |J₀(β)|. J₀ is zero at β = 2.405 (the computed value there is about -9 × 10⁻⁵ ≈ 0), so at that modulation index all of the power is in the sidebands. The zeros of J₀ at 2.405, 5.52, 8.65 are used to calibrate frequency deviation (the Bessel-null method).\n\n**Q5.** **Bandwidth**: AM needs 2f_m (narrow); wideband FM needs 2(Δf + f_m), about 12f_m for β = 5 (broadcast FM: 180 kHz against 10 kHz for AM).\n**Power efficiency**: AM wastes at least 67 percent of its power in the carrier (efficiency at most 33 percent); FM is constant envelope, so all power is useful and efficient class C nonlinear amplifiers can be used.\n**Noise**: FM gives a noise reduction (\"FM improvement\") proportional to β² above a threshold SNR (about 10 dB), at the cost of bandwidth; AM has no such trade and its envelope is directly corrupted by noise and interference; FM also rejects amplitude noise (limiter) and shows the capture effect.\n**Complexity**: AM demodulation is simplest (envelope detector, one diode); FM needs a discriminator or PLL.\n**Uses**: AM for medium-wave broadcasting and aviation voice; FM for high-quality broadcasting, two-way radio and analogue TV sound.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Step 1: SQNR = 6.02n + 1.76 dB.\nStep 2: n = 8: 48.16 + 1.76 = **49.9 dB**; n = 12: 72.24 + 1.76 = **74.0 dB**; n = 16: 96.32 + 1.76 = **98.1 dB**; n = 24: 144.48 + 1.76 = **146.2 dB**.\n(b) Step 1: solve n = (SQNR - 1.76)/6.02. For 40 dB: (40 - 1.76)/6.02 = 6.35, round up to **7 bits**.\nStep 2: for 60 dB: (60 - 1.76)/6.02 = 9.67, round up to **10 bits**.\n(c) Step 1: bit rate = sampling rate × bits per sample (× channels). Telephone: 8000 × 8 = **64 kb/s**.\nStep 2: CD stereo: 44,100 × 16 × 2 = **1,411,200 b/s = 1.4112 Mb/s**.\n(d) T1: 24 channels × 64 kb/s = 1536 kb/s, plus 8 kb/s framing: **1544 kb/s**.\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) Step 1: an AM spectrum has a carrier and two sidebands at f_c ± f_m.\nStep 2: the carrier is at 100 kHz with amplitude **1.0** (normalised); the sidebands are at 100 - 5 = **95 kHz** and 100 + 5 = **105 kHz**, each of amplitude mA_c/2 = 0.8/2 = **0.4**.\n(b) Step 1: β = Δf/f_m = 50/10 = 5.\nStep 2: Bessel function amplitudes: carrier |J₀(5)| = **0.1776**; first sideband pair |J₁(5)| = **0.3276**; second |J₂(5)| = **0.0466**; third |J₃(5)| = **0.3648**. (The simulated FFT amplitudes matched these values.)\n(c) Step 1: the power in the carrier and n sideband pairs is J₀² + 2ΣJₙ² (n = 1 to k).\nStep 2: cumulative power for β = 5: k = 3: 0.517; k = 4: 0.823; k = 5: 0.959; k = 6: 0.994.\nStep 3: 98 percent is first exceeded at k = 6: **6 pairs**, a bandwidth of 12f_m, as Carson's rule 2(β + 1)f_m = 12f_m predicts.\n\n# Assignment 2: Digital Modulation and Channel Capacity (Chapters 4 and 5)\n\n**Q1. BER theory.** BER = Q(sqrt(2 Eb/N0)) for BPSK. Find the Eb/N0 (dB) needed for BER 10^-3, 10^-5 and 10^-6 [10]\n\n**Q2. BPSK and QPSK.** A simulation of 2 million bits gave BPSK BER 0.0374 at 2 dB, 0.01251 at 4 dB, 0.002431 at 6 dB and 0.000172 at 8 dB. (a) Compare with theory at these points [5] (b) Explain why QPSK has the same BER per bit as BPSK [3] (c) What does QPSK gain? [2]\n\n**Q3. 16-QAM.** (a) Number of bits per symbol and Eb/Es relation for the constellation with levels ±1, ±3 on each axis [3] (b) The approximate BER 0.75 Q(sqrt(0.8 Eb/N0)): the Eb/N0 required for 10^-3 and 10^-5 [4] (c) Simulation gave 0.0279 at 6 dB, 0.00922 at 8 dB and 0.00177 at 10 dB: compare [3]\n\n**Q4. Bandwidth.** (a) A QPSK link with Rs = 1 Msymbol/s and raised-cosine roll-off 0.35: the bandwidth, the bit rate and the spectral efficiency [4] (b) 64-QAM with Rs = 10 Msymbols/s and alpha = 0.2: the bit rate and bandwidth [3] (c) A baseband channel of 4 kHz and alpha = 0.25: the maximum symbol rate [3]\n\n**Q5. Shannon capacity.** Find C for (a) 1 MHz at 20 dB [2] (b) 20 MHz at 25 dB [2] (c) 3100 Hz at 35 dB [2] (d) 5 MHz at 10 dB [2] (e) the SNR needed for 4 b/s/Hz, and the Shannon limit on Eb/N0 [2]\n\n**Q6. Detection principles.** (a) What is a matched filter and why does it maximise the SNR? [4] (b) What is intersymbol interference and the Nyquist criterion? [3] (c) Why is Gray coding used? [3]\n\n**Q7. Constellations.** (a) For QPSK with symbol energy Es express the minimum distance and the energy per bit [4] (b) For 16-QAM with levels ±1 and ±3 compute the average symbol energy, Eb and the minimum distance [4] (c) Why does a larger constellation need more SNR? [2]\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 2\n\n**Q1.** BPSK: BER = Q(√(2E_b/N₀)). Invert: let x = Q⁻¹(BER); then E_b/N₀ = x²/2.\nStep 1 (BER = 10⁻³): x = 3.090; E_b/N₀ = 3.090²/2 = 4.775; in dB: 10 log₁₀ 4.775 = **6.79 dB**.\nStep 2 (BER = 10⁻⁵): x = 4.265; E_b/N₀ = 4.265²/2 = 9.095; **9.59 dB**.\nStep 3 (BER = 10⁻⁶): x = 4.753; E_b/N₀ = 4.753²/2 = 11.30; **10.53 dB**.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Step 1: theoretical BPSK BER = Q(√(2E_b/N₀)) with E_b/N₀ = 10^(dB/10).\nStep 2: 2 dB (1.585): Q(√3.17) = Q(1.78) = **0.0375**; 4 dB (2.512): Q(2.24) = **0.01250**; 6 dB (3.981): Q(2.82) = **0.00239**; 8 dB (6.310): Q(3.55) = **0.000191**.\nStep 3: the simulated values 0.0374, 0.01251, 0.002431 and 0.000172 agree within the Monte Carlo error (at 8 dB only about 340 errors were counted in 2 × 10⁶ bits, so the statistical uncertainty is about 5 percent).\n(b) Step 1: QPSK is two independent BPSK signals on the in-phase and quadrature carriers, which are orthogonal.\nStep 2: each bit uses half the symbol energy, E_b = E_s/2, and the noise in the two quadratures is independent, so each bit has the same decision statistic as BPSK: BER = Q(√(2E_b/N₀)). The simulation confirmed it: 0.01256 at 4 dB, 0.00241 at 6 dB, 0.000212 at 8 dB.\n(c) QPSK sends twice the bit rate in the same bandwidth (twice the spectral efficiency) with no loss of BER per bit.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Step 1: 16 points, so log₂ 16 = **4 bits per symbol** (2 bits on each axis).\nStep 2: mean square level per axis = (1² + 3² + (-1)² + (-3)²)/4 = (1 + 9 + 1 + 9)/4 = 5.\nStep 3: E_s = 2 × 5 = **10** (two axes) and E_b = E_s/4 = **2.5**.\n(b) Step 1: 0.75 Q(√(0.8x)) = 10⁻³, so Q(√(0.8x)) = 1.333 × 10⁻³ and √(0.8x) = 3.004.\nStep 2: 0.8x = 9.02, so x = 11.28 = **10.52 dB**.\nStep 3: for 10⁻⁵: Q(·) = 1.333 × 10⁻⁵, argument 4.200, 0.8x = 17.64, x = 22.05 = **13.43 dB**.\nStep 4: compared with BPSK (6.79 and 9.59 dB), 16-QAM needs about 3.7 dB more E_b/N₀ for four times the bits per symbol.\n(c) Step 1: approximation at 6 dB (3.981): 0.75 Q(√3.185) = 0.75 Q(1.785) = 0.0279; at 8 dB: 0.00925; at 10 dB: 0.00175.\nStep 2: simulation: 0.0279, 0.00922, 0.00177: excellent agreement.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Step 1: bandwidth of a raised-cosine pulse: B = (1 + α)R_s = 1.35 × 1 MHz = **1.35 MHz**.\nStep 2: QPSK sends 2 bits per symbol: R_b = 2R_s = **2 Mb/s**.\nStep 3: spectral efficiency = 2 Mb/s / 1.35 MHz = **1.48 b/s/Hz**.\n(b) Step 1: 64-QAM carries log₂ 64 = 6 bits per symbol: R_b = 6 × 10 = **60 Mb/s**.\nStep 2: B = (1 + 0.2) × 10 MHz = **12 MHz** (5 b/s/Hz).\n(c) Step 1: for a baseband channel of bandwidth B, B = (1 + α)R_s/2.\nStep 2: R_s = 2B/(1 + α) = 2 × 4000/1.25 = **6400 symbols/s** (the ideal Nyquist rate 2B = 8000 is reached only with α = 0).\n\n**Q5.** C = B log₂(1 + SNR), with SNR as a ratio: SNR = 10^(dB/10).\n(a) SNR = 100: C = 10⁶ log₂ 101 = 10⁶ × 6.658 = **6.66 Mb/s**.\n(b) SNR = 316.2: C = 20 × 10⁶ × log₂ 317.2 = 20 × 10⁶ × 8.309 = **166.2 Mb/s**.\n(c) SNR = 3162: C = 3100 × log₂ 3163 = 3100 × 11.63 = **36.0 kb/s**.\n(d) SNR = 10: C = 5 × 10⁶ × log₂ 11 = 5 × 10⁶ × 3.459 = **17.3 Mb/s**.\n(e) Step 1: for spectral efficiency 4 b/s/Hz: SNR = 2⁴ - 1 = 15 = 10 log₁₀ 15 = **11.76 dB**.\nStep 2: the Shannon limit (infinite bandwidth) on E_b/N₀ is ln 2 = 0.693, which is **-1.59 dB**.\n\n**Q6.** (a) A **matched filter** has impulse response h(t) = s(T - t), the time-reversed signal; its output at t = T is the correlation of the received signal with the expected pulse. By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality it gives the maximum output SNR, 2E/N₀ for white noise (the energy-to-noise-density ratio), so it is the optimum detector for AWGN.\n(b) Step 1: **ISI**: dispersion makes the response of one symbol overlap neighbouring symbol periods.\nStep 2: **Nyquist criterion**: the overall pulse p(t) must satisfy p(kT) = 0 for non-zero integers k (and 1 for k = 0); in the frequency domain the sum of the spectrum shifted by multiples of 1/T must be constant.\nStep 3: The minimum bandwidth is 1/(2T), reached by the sinc pulse; raised-cosine pulses trade extra bandwidth for faster-decaying tails.\n(c) With **Gray coding** neighbouring constellation points differ in exactly one bit, so the most likely symbol error (to a neighbour) causes only one bit error: BER ≈ SER/log₂ M.\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) Step 1: QPSK points (±a, ±a): E_s = a² + a² = 2a².\nStep 2: the minimum distance d = 2a = √(2E_s) (using a = √(E_s/2)).\nStep 3: two bits per symbol, so E_b = E_s/2.\n(b) Step 1: 16-QAM levels ±1, ±3: the mean square level per axis is 5, so average symbol energy E_s = **10**.\nStep 2: E_b = E_s/4 = **2.5**.\nStep 3: minimum distance (adjacent levels differ by 2) d = **2**.\nStep 4: d²/E_b = 4/2.5 = 1.6, compared with 4 for BPSK/QPSK: 2.5 times smaller (4 dB), which is why 16-QAM needs more SNR.\n(c) For a given average energy the points of a bigger constellation are closer together (smaller minimum distance), so a smaller noise amplitude causes decision errors; the SNR must grow by roughly 6 dB for each additional bit per symbol in QAM.\n\n# Assignment 3: Coding, OFDM, Spread Spectrum and Link Budgets (Chapters 6 to 8)\n\n**Q1. Hamming (7,4).** With parity p1 = d1 + d2 + d4, p2 = d1 + d3 + d4, p3 = d2 + d3 + d4 and codeword [d1 d2 d3 d4 p1 p2 p3]: (a) Encode 1011 [3] (b) Decode the received word 1001010 [4] (c) Decode the received word 1011000 (the codeword 1011010 with its 6th bit flipped) and explain the syndrome method [3]\n\n**Q2. Code capability.** (a) The minimum distance and rate of the Hamming (7,4) code, and its detection and correction capability [4] (b) How many errors can a code with d_min = 5 detect and correct? [3] (c) What does an exhaustive test of all single errors show? [3]\n\n**Q3. Convolutional code.** For the rate-1/2 K = 3 encoder with c1 = b + s1 + s2 and c2 = b + s2: (a) Encode 1011 followed by two zeros [4] (b) Describe how the Viterbi decoder corrects two errors [3] (c) What BER did simulation give at p = 0.01, 0.03 and 0.05? [3]\n\n**Q4. OFDM.** (a) Describe the role of the cyclic prefix [3] (b) The simulation with 64 subcarriers and a 4-tap channel: results with and without the prefix [3] (c) Wi-Fi 802.11a: the subcarrier spacing, symbol duration and the 54 Mb/s rate [4]\n\n**Q5. Spread spectrum.** (a) The length and autocorrelation of a 7-stage m-sequence and its processing gain [4] (b) Show how Walsh codes separate two CDMA users [4] (c) What is frequency hopping? [2]\n\n**Q6. Link budget.** (a) Compute the free-space path loss for 36,000 km at 4 GHz, 1 km at 2.4 GHz, 10 km at 900 MHz, 20 km at 2 GHz and 384,400 km at 2.2 GHz [5] (b) A 20 dBm transmitter with 3 dBi antennas at both ends over 1 km at 2.4 GHz: the received power, the noise floor (20 MHz, NF 6 dB) and the SNR [3] (c) The maximum range for a path loss of 110 dB at 2.4 GHz [2]\n\n**Q7. Multiple access.** (a) Compare FDMA, TDMA, CDMA and OFDMA [5] (b) Explain frequency reuse in cellular networks [3] (c) What is the benefit of MIMO? [2]\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 3\n\n**Q1.** Parity rules: p₁ = d₁ ⊕ d₂ ⊕ d₄, p₂ = d₁ ⊕ d₃ ⊕ d₄, p₃ = d₂ ⊕ d₃ ⊕ d₄ (⊕ = addition mod 2); codeword [d₁ d₂ d₃ d₄ p₁ p₂ p₃].\n(a) Data 1011 means d₁ = 1, d₂ = 0, d₃ = 1, d₄ = 1.\nStep 1: p₁ = 1 + 0 + 1 = 2 → **0**.\nStep 2: p₂ = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 → **1**.\nStep 3: p₃ = 0 + 1 + 1 = 2 → **0**.\nStep 4: codeword **1011010**.\n(b) Received r = 1001010: d₁ = 1, d₂ = 0, d₃ = 0, d₄ = 1, p₁ = 0, p₂ = 1, p₃ = 0.\nStep 1: syndrome bits s₁ = d₁ + d₂ + d₄ + p₁ = 1 + 0 + 1 + 0 = 2 → 0.\nStep 2: s₂ = d₁ + d₃ + d₄ + p₂ = 1 + 0 + 1 + 1 = 3 → 1.\nStep 3: s₃ = d₂ + d₃ + d₄ + p₃ = 0 + 0 + 1 + 0 = 1.\nStep 4: syndrome [0, 1, 1]. The bit that takes part in checks 2 and 3 only is d₃ (bit 3), so the error is in bit 3: flip it to get 1011010.\nStep 5: the data are **1011**.\n(c) Received 1011000 (the codeword 1011010 with bit 6 flipped): d = 1, 0, 1, 1; p₁ = 0, p₂ = 0, p₃ = 0.\nStep 1: s₁ = 1 + 0 + 1 + 0 = 2 → 0; s₂ = 1 + 1 + 1 + 0 = 3 → 1; s₃ = 0 + 1 + 1 + 0 = 2 → 0. Syndrome [0, 1, 0].\nStep 2: [0, 1, 0] is the column belonging to p₂ (bit 6). Flip bit 6: 1011010 ✓.\nMethod: compute s = H r (mod 2); if s = 0 accept; otherwise find the column of H equal to s: that bit is in error.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Step 1: compute the Hamming distance between all pairs of the 16 codewords: the smallest non-zero distance is **d_min = 3**.\nStep 2: rate = k/n = **4/7 = 0.571**.\nStep 3: detection capability = d_min - 1 = **2** errors; correction capability = ⌊(d_min - 1)/2⌋ = **1** error.\n(b) d_min = 5: detects 5 - 1 = **4** errors and corrects ⌊(5 - 1)/2⌋ = **2** errors.\n(c) Testing all 16 messages × 7 single-error positions = 112 cases, **112 of 112** were corrected: a perfect code, since each of the 2³ - 1 = 7 non-zero syndromes corresponds to exactly one single-bit error.\n\n**Q3.** Encoder: c₁ = b ⊕ s₁ ⊕ s₂, c₂ = b ⊕ s₂, with state (s₁, s₂) starting at (0, 0); after each bit the state becomes (b, s₁).\n(a) Step 1 (b = 1, state 00): c₁ = 1, c₂ = 1 → **11**; new state (1, 0).\nStep 2 (b = 0, state 10): c₁ = 0 + 1 + 0 = 1, c₂ = 0 + 0 = 0 → **10**; state (0, 1).\nStep 3 (b = 1, state 01): c₁ = 1 + 0 + 1 = 0, c₂ = 1 + 1 = 0 → **00**; state (1, 0).\nStep 4 (b = 1, state 10): c₁ = 1 + 1 + 0 = 0, c₂ = 1 + 0 = 1 → **01**; state (1, 1).\nStep 5 (b = 0, flush, state 11): c₁ = 0 + 1 + 1 = 0, c₂ = 0 + 1 = 1 → **01**; state (0, 1).\nStep 6 (b = 0, flush, state 01): c₁ = 0 + 0 + 1 = 1, c₂ = 0 + 1 = 1 → **11**; state (0, 0).\nOutput stream: 11 10 00 01 01 11 = 1 1 1 0 0 0 0 1 0 1 1 1.\n(b) The Viterbi decoder keeps, for each of the 4 trellis states, the path with the smallest Hamming distance to the received bits; at the end it picks the state with the smallest metric and traces back. With two received bits flipped (the 4th and 9th), the correct path still has the smallest total distance (**metric 2**, the two corrupted bits) because any other path differs from the transmitted one in at least 5 coded bits (the free distance of this code is 5), so the decoded data were **1 0 1 1 0 0**.\n(c) Simulated output BER after decoding: p = 0.01: **5 × 10⁻⁵**; p = 0.03: **1.6 × 10⁻³**; p = 0.05: **5.9 × 10⁻³**, against uncoded 0.01, 0.03, 0.05.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) The cyclic prefix (a copy of the end of each symbol placed in front) absorbs the delay spread of the multipath channel so that echoes of one symbol do not leak into the next, and it makes the channel look like a circular convolution: the receiver's FFT then sees H(k)X(k) on every subcarrier, which a one-tap equaliser inverts. The prefix must be longer than the delay spread; it costs bandwidth and energy (16/64 = 25 percent overhead here).\n(b) With a 16-sample prefix, channel h = [1, 0.5, 0.3j, 0.2], no noise, 2000 symbols × 64 subcarriers × 2 bits = 256,000 bits: **0 errors**. Without the prefix: **487 errors**.\n(c) Step 1: subcarrier spacing = sampling rate / FFT size = 20 MHz/64 = **312.5 kHz**.\nStep 2: useful symbol duration = 64/20 MHz = **3.2 μs**; adding the 0.8 μs guard interval gives **4.0 μs**.\nStep 3: rate = 48 data subcarriers × 6 bits (64-QAM) × (3/4 code rate) / 4 μs = 216 bits/4 μs = **54 Mb/s**.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Step 1: a 7-stage LFSR gives a maximal-length sequence of 2⁷ - 1 = **127 chips**.\nStep 2: the periodic autocorrelation is **127** at zero shift and **-1** at every other shift (verified numerically).\nStep 3: other shifts, other users, noise and jammers are 127 times (21 dB) weaker than the wanted peak: processing gain = 10 log₁₀ 127 = **21.0 dB**.\n(b) Rows of the 8 × 8 Hadamard matrix are orthogonal (HHᵀ = 8I). User 1 uses row 1, user 2 uses row 2. If the users send bits (1, -1, 1) and (-1, -1, 1) at the same time, the received signal is the sum of the two spread signals. Correlating each bit period with row 1 and dividing by 8 returns **1, -1, 1** exactly (the contribution of user 2 correlates to zero); correlating with row 2 returns user 2's data. This works only when the users are chip-synchronised (otherwise PN codes with low cross-correlation are used).\n(c) **Frequency hopping**: the carrier frequency changes pseudo-randomly among many channels (Bluetooth: 1600 hops/s over 79 channels); it resists narrowband interference and eavesdropping.\n\n**Q6.** Free-space path loss: FSPL(dB) = 20 log₁₀(d_km) + 20 log₁₀(f_MHz) + 32.44.\n(a) Geostationary satellite, d = 36,000 km, f = 4000 MHz: 91.1 + 72.0 + 32.44 = **195.6 dB**.\n1 km at 2400 MHz: 0 + 67.6 + 32.44 = **100.0 dB**.\n10 km at 900 MHz: 20 + 59.1 + 32.44 = **111.5 dB**.\n20 km at 2000 MHz: 26.0 + 66.0 + 32.44 = **124.5 dB**.\nMoon, 384,400 km at 2200 MHz: 111.7 + 66.8 + 32.44 = **211.0 dB**.\n(b) Step 1: received power = 20 dBm + 3 dBi + 3 dBi - 100.0 dB = **-74.0 dBm**.\nStep 2: noise floor = -174 + 10 log₁₀(20 × 10⁶) + 6 = -174 + 73.0 + 6 = **-95.0 dBm**.\nStep 3: SNR = -74.0 - (-95.0) = **21.0 dB**.\n(c) Step 1: 110 = 20 log₁₀ d + 67.6 + 32.44, so 20 log₁₀ d = 110 - 100.04 = 9.96.\nStep 2: d = 10^(9.96/20) = **3.15 km** (free space; real obstacles shorten it).\n\n**Q7.** (a) **FDMA**: each user has its own frequency band (analog cellular, radio): simple, needs guard bands. **TDMA**: users share a carrier in time slots (GSM: 8 slots): needs synchronisation, flexible rates. **CDMA**: users share time and frequency but are separated by orthogonal or PN codes (3G, GPS): soft capacity, rake receivers, power control needed. **OFDMA**: groups of subcarriers are assigned to users in time and frequency (LTE, 5G, Wi-Fi 6): flexible, efficient scheduling, robust to multipath.\n(b) The spectrum is divided into groups of channels; the same channel set is reused in cells far enough apart (reuse distance) that co-channel interference is acceptable. A cluster of N cells (N = 3, 4, 7) gives each cell 1/N of the channels; smaller cells give more capacity per area at the price of more base stations and handovers.\n(c) **MIMO** (multiple antennas at both ends) exploits multipath: spatial multiplexing sends independent streams (capacity grows roughly with min(N_t, N_r) at the same bandwidth and power), and diversity and beamforming improve reliability and SNR.\n","exam":"# Communication Systems: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** The noise power in a 1 MHz bandwidth at 290 K is about\nA) -174 dBm  B) -144 dBm  C) -114 dBm  D) -84 dBm\n\n**2.** In AM, the sideband power for carrier power Pc and modulation index m is\nA) Pc m^2/2  B) Pc m  C) Pc/m  D) 2 Pc\n\n**3.** The envelope detector requires a time constant RC such that\nA) RC >> 1/fm  B) RC << 1/fc  C) RC = 1/fc  D) 1/fc << RC << 1/fm\n\n**4.** Which AM variant has the narrowest bandwidth?\nA) DSB-SC  B) conventional AM  C) SSB  D) FM\n\n**5.** An FM signal with delta_f = 75 kHz and fm = 15 kHz has a modulation index of\nA) 0.2  B) 5  C) 15  D) 75\n\n**6.** The carrier component of a single-tone FM wave is proportional to\nA) J1(beta)  B) beta  C) J0(beta)  D) 1/beta\n\n**7.** The SQNR of an ideal 12-bit quantiser is about\nA) 49.9 dB  B) 62 dB  C) 74 dB  D) 98 dB\n\n**8.** The Nyquist criterion for zero ISI requires a pulse that\nA) has zero value at the other sampling instants  B) is rectangular  C) has infinite energy  D) is periodic\n\n**9.** The BER of 16-QAM at a given Eb/N0 is\nA) lower than QPSK  B) the same as QPSK  C) higher than QPSK  D) zero\n\n**10.** A matched filter\nA) maximises the SNR at the sampling instant  B) removes all noise  C) is a high-pass filter  D) has a flat response\n\n**11.** Gray coding gives\nA) the minimum bandwidth  B) one bit difference between adjacent constellation points  C) the maximum power  D) zero ISI\n\n**12.** For a channel with SNR = 1 (0 dB) the capacity per hertz is\nA) 0 b/s/Hz  B) 0.5 b/s/Hz  C) 1 b/s/Hz  D) 2 b/s/Hz\n\n**13.** The minimum Eb/N0 for reliable communication is\nA) -1.59 dB  B) 0 dB  C) 3 dB  D) 6 dB\n\n**14.** A code with minimum distance 3 can\nA) correct 1 error  B) correct 2 errors  C) detect 3 errors  D) correct 3 errors\n\n**15.** The syndrome of a received Hamming word identifies\nA) the data  B) the position of a single error  C) the CRC  D) the carrier\n\n**16.** The Viterbi algorithm finds\nA) the shortest path in a network  B) the maximum-likelihood path through the trellis  C) the generator polynomial  D) the interleaver\n\n**17.** The cyclic prefix in OFDM must be at least as long as\nA) the symbol  B) the sampling period  C) the delay spread of the channel  D) the carrier period\n\n**18.** A 63-chip PN sequence gives a processing gain of about\nA) 6 dB  B) 12 dB  C) 18 dB  D) 63 dB\n\n**19.** Free-space path loss increases by about how much when the distance is doubled?\nA) 3 dB  B) 6 dB  C) 12 dB  D) 20 dB\n\n**20.** OFDMA is the multiple-access scheme of\nA) AMPS  B) GSM  C) IS-95  D) LTE and 5G\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** An AM transmitter sends 1000 W in total with m = 0.6. Find the carrier power, the sideband power, the efficiency and the bandwidth for a 5 kHz message.\n\n**B2. (5)** FM broadcast: delta_f = 75 kHz and fm = 15 kHz. Find beta and the Carson bandwidth. What is the carrier amplitude relative to the unmodulated carrier when beta = 5?\n\n**B3. (5)** How many bits are needed for an SQNR of 60 dB? Find the bit rates of telephone speech (8 kHz, 8 bits) and of CD stereo (44.1 kHz, 16 bits).\n\n**B4. (5)** Find the Eb/N0 in dB at which BPSK achieves BER = 10^-5, and the Eb/N0 at which 16-QAM achieves 10^-3 using 0.75 Q(sqrt(0.8 Eb/N0)).\n\n**B5. (5)** A QPSK link has Rs = 1 Msymbol/s and a raised-cosine roll-off of 0.35. Find the bandwidth, the bit rate and the spectral efficiency.\n\n**B6. (5)** Find the capacity for B = 20 MHz and SNR = 25 dB, and the SNR required for 4 b/s/Hz.\n\n**B7. (5)** Encode the data 1011 with the Hamming (7,4) code (p1 = d1 + d2 + d4, p2 = d1 + d3 + d4, p3 = d2 + d3 + d4) and decode the received word 1001010.\n\n**B8. (5)** Compute the free-space path loss for 10 km at 900 MHz and the received power for Pt = 30 dBm, Gt = 10 dBi and Gr = 0 dBi.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. AM and FM.** (a) The envelope of an AM wave has maximum 12 V and minimum 4 V: m, the carrier amplitude and the sideband amplitude [3]. (b) The measured AM spectrum lines for fc = 100 kHz, fm = 5 kHz, m = 0.8 [2]. (c) FM tone with fc = 500 kHz, fm = 10 kHz, delta_f = 50 kHz: beta, the first four line amplitudes and the number of sideband pairs holding 98 percent of the power [3]. (d) Compare AM and wideband FM [2].\n\n**C2. Digital modulation and BER.** (a) BPSK BER = Q(sqrt(2 Eb/N0)): compute the simulated and theoretical values at 4, 6 and 8 dB (simulation: 0.01251, 0.002431, 0.000172) [4]. (b) Why does QPSK match BPSK per bit? [2] (c) 16-QAM: bits per symbol, Es and Eb for levels ±1, ±3, and the Eb/N0 for 10^-3 [4].\n\n**C3. Capacity and coding.** (a) Shannon capacity examples for 1 MHz at 20 dB and 3100 Hz at 35 dB; the Shannon limit [3]. (b) Hamming (7,4): d_min, correction capability, the result of the exhaustive single-error test [3]. (c) The rate-1/2 K = 3 convolutional code (c1 = b + s1 + s2, c2 = b + s2): encode 1011 with 2 tail zeros; the Viterbi result with 2 channel errors and the BER at p = 0.01 [4].\n\n**C4. OFDM and spread spectrum.** (a) The role of the cyclic prefix; simulation results with 64 subcarriers: 0 errors with the prefix and 487 of 256,000 without [3]. (b) 802.11a: subcarrier spacing, symbol time and the 54 Mb/s rate [3]. (c) A 127-chip m-sequence: autocorrelation values and processing gain; two CDMA users with Walsh codes [4].\n\n**C5. Link budgets and multiple access.** (a) FSPL for 36,000 km at 4 GHz, 1 km at 2.4 GHz and 20 km at 2 GHz [3]. (b) A 20 dBm transmitter with 3 dBi antennas at both ends over 1 km at 2.4 GHz: Pr, noise floor (20 MHz, NF 6 dB) and SNR [3]. (c) The maximum range for a 110 dB path loss at 2.4 GHz [2]. (d) Compare FDMA, TDMA, CDMA and OFDMA [2].\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **C** · 2 **A** · 3 **D** · 4 **C** · 5 **B** · 6 **C** · 7 **C** · 8 **A** · 9 **C** · 10 **A** · 11 **B** · 12 **C** · 13 **A** · 14 **A** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **C** · 18 **C** · 19 **B** · 20 **D**\n\nBrief reasons:\n1. Step 1: N = kTB; in dBm: -174 + 10 log₁₀ B(Hz). Step 2: 10 log₁₀(10⁶) = 60, so N = -174 + 60 = -114 dBm.\n2. Step 1: each sideband carries P_c m²/4. Step 2: the two sidebands together carry P_c m²/2.\n3. Step 1: the capacitor must follow the envelope but not the carrier. Step 2: so 1/f_c ≪ RC ≪ 1/f_m.\n4. SSB transmits only one sideband, so its bandwidth is f_m, the narrowest of the AM variants (DSB-SC and AM need 2f_m).\n5. Step 1: β = Δf/f_m. Step 2: = 75/15 = 5.\n6. The carrier component of a single-tone FM wave is proportional to J₀(β).\n7. Step 1: SQNR = 6.02n + 1.76. Step 2: = 6.02 × 12 + 1.76 = 72.24 + 1.76 = 74.0 dB.\n8. The Nyquist criterion for zero ISI requires a pulse that is zero at all other sampling instants.\n9. At the same E_b/N₀ 16-QAM has points closer together, so its BER is higher than QPSK.\n10. A matched filter maximises the SNR at the sampling instant.\n11. Gray coding makes adjacent constellation points differ in one bit.\n12. Step 1: C/B = log₂(1 + SNR). Step 2: SNR = 1: log₂ 2 = 1 b/s/Hz.\n13. The minimum E_b/N₀ for reliable communication (Shannon limit) is ln 2 = 0.693, that is -1.59 dB.\n14. Step 1: correctable errors t = ⌊(d_min - 1)/2⌋. Step 2: ⌊(3 - 1)/2⌋ = 1; the code can detect 2.\n15. The syndrome identifies the position of a single-bit error.\n16. The Viterbi algorithm finds the maximum-likelihood path through the trellis.\n17. The cyclic prefix must be at least as long as the delay spread of the channel.\n18. Step 1: processing gain = 10 log₁₀(chips). Step 2: 10 log₁₀ 63 = 18.0 dB.\n19. Step 1: FSPL ∝ 20 log₁₀ d. Step 2: doubling the distance adds 20 log₁₀ 2 = 6.02 dB.\n20. OFDMA is the multiple-access scheme of LTE and 5G.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** Total power 1000 W, m = 0.6.\nStep 1: carrier power P_c = P_t/(1 + m²/2) = 1000/(1 + 0.18) = **847.5 W**.\nStep 2: sideband power = 1000 - 847.5 = **152.5 W**.\nStep 3: efficiency η = m²/(2 + m²) = 0.36/2.36 = **15.3 percent**.\nStep 4: bandwidth = 2f_m = 2 × 5 = **10 kHz**.\n\n**B2.** Step 1: β = Δf/f_m = 75/15 = **5**.\nStep 2: Carson bandwidth = 2(Δf + f_m) = 2(75 + 15) = **180 kHz**.\nStep 3: carrier amplitude relative to the unmodulated carrier = |J₀(5)| = **0.178** (carrier power fraction J₀² = 0.032 = 3.2 percent).\n\n**B3.** Step 1: SQNR = 6.02n + 1.76 ≥ 60, so n ≥ (60 - 1.76)/6.02 = 9.67, i.e. **10 bits**.\nStep 2: telephone: 8000 × 8 = **64 kb/s**.\nStep 3: CD stereo: 44,100 × 16 × 2 = **1,411,200 b/s = 1.4112 Mb/s**.\n\n**B4.** Step 1 (BPSK, 10⁻⁵): Q(x) = 10⁻⁵ gives x = 4.265; E_b/N₀ = x²/2 = 9.09; in dB **9.59 dB**.\nStep 2 (16-QAM, 10⁻³): 0.75 Q(√(0.8x)) = 10⁻³ gives Q = 1.333 × 10⁻³ and √(0.8x) = 3.004.\nStep 3: x = 3.004²/0.8 = 11.28; in dB **10.52 dB**.\n\n**B5.** Step 1: bandwidth = (1 + α)R_s = 1.35 × 1 MHz = **1.35 MHz**.\nStep 2: QPSK carries 2 bits per symbol: bit rate = 2 × 1 = **2 Mb/s**.\nStep 3: spectral efficiency = 2/1.35 = **1.48 b/s/Hz**.\n\n**B6.** Step 1: 25 dB → SNR = 10^2.5 = 316.2.\nStep 2: C = B log₂(1 + SNR) = 20 × 10⁶ × log₂(317.2) = 20 × 10⁶ × 8.309 = **166.2 Mb/s**.\nStep 3: 4 b/s/Hz: 1 + SNR = 2⁴ = 16, so SNR = 15 = 10 log₁₀ 15 = **11.76 dB**.\n\n**B7.** Encode d = 1011 (d₁ = 1, d₂ = 0, d₃ = 1, d₄ = 1):\nStep 1: p₁ = 1 + 0 + 1 = 2 → 0; p₂ = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 → 1; p₃ = 0 + 1 + 1 = 2 → 0. Codeword **1011010**.\nDecode r = 1001010 (d = 1001, p = 010):\nStep 2: s₁ = d₁ + d₂ + d₄ + p₁ = 1 + 0 + 1 + 0 = 2 → 0.\nStep 3: s₂ = d₁ + d₃ + d₄ + p₂ = 1 + 0 + 1 + 1 = 3 → 1.\nStep 4: s₃ = d₂ + d₃ + d₄ + p₃ = 0 + 0 + 1 + 0 = 1.\nStep 5: syndrome **011**, the column of the 3rd bit (d₃, which appears in checks 2 and 3 only). Flip bit 3: 1011010, so the data are **1011**.\n\n**B8.** Step 1: FSPL = 20 log₁₀(10) + 20 log₁₀(900) + 32.44 = 20 + 59.08 + 32.44 = **111.5 dB**.\nStep 2: P_r = P_t + G_t + G_r - FSPL = 30 + 10 + 0 - 111.5 = **-71.5 dBm** (a margin of 28.5 dB over a receiver sensitivity of -100 dBm).\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.**\n(a) Step 1: A_c(1 + m) = 12 and A_c(1 - m) = 4. Adding: 2A_c = 16, so **A_c = 8 V**.\nStep 2: m = (12 - 4)/(12 + 4) = 8/16 = **0.5**.\nStep 3: sideband amplitude = mA_c/2 = 0.5 × 8/2 = **2 V**.\n(b) Lines at the carrier 100 kHz (amplitude **1.0**) and the sidebands 95 kHz and 105 kHz (each mA_c/2 = 0.8/2 = **0.4**).\n(c) Step 1: β = Δf/f_m = 50/10 = **5**.\nStep 2: line amplitudes at the carrier and first three sidebands: |J₀(5)| = **0.1776**, |J₁(5)| = **0.3276**, |J₂(5)| = **0.0466**, |J₃(5)| = **0.3648** (the FFT of the simulated signal matched these).\nStep 3: the cumulative power in the carrier plus k sideband pairs for β = 5 is 0.517 (k = 3), 0.823 (k = 4), 0.959 (k = 5), 0.994 (k = 6): 98 percent is first reached at **6 sideband pairs** (bandwidth 12f_m = 120 kHz, as Carson's rule 2(β + 1)f_m gives).\n(d) AM: narrow (2f_m), simple receivers, low efficiency (at most 33 percent), noise directly affects the envelope. Wideband FM: wide bandwidth (180 kHz against 10 kHz), constant envelope (efficient amplifiers), noise improvement proportional to β² above the threshold, more complex demodulators.\n\n**C2.**\n(a) Step 1: theory Q(√(2E_b/N₀)): 4 dB (2.512): Q(√5.02) = Q(2.24) = **0.01250**.\nStep 2: 6 dB (3.981): Q(√7.96) = Q(2.82) = **0.002388**.\nStep 3: 8 dB (6.310): Q(√12.62) = Q(3.55) = **0.000191**.\nStep 4: simulated 0.01251, 0.002431, 0.000172: they agree within the Monte Carlo error (at 8 dB only about 340 errors were counted).\n(b) QPSK is two orthogonal BPSK streams (in-phase and quadrature) with independent noise; each bit has energy E_b and the same distance-to-noise ratio as BPSK, so the BER per bit is identical; QPSK sends two bits per symbol in the same bandwidth that BPSK uses for one.\n(c) Step 1: 16-QAM carries **4 bits per symbol**.\nStep 2: mean square level per axis = (1 + 9 + 1 + 9)/4 = 5; E_s = 2 × 5 = **10**; E_b = E_s/4 = **2.5**.\nStep 3: 0.75 Q(√(0.8E_b/N₀)) = 10⁻³ requires E_b/N₀ = 11.28 = **10.52 dB** (BPSK: 6.79 dB).\nStep 4: simulated BERs of 0.0279, 0.00922 and 0.00177 at 6, 8 and 10 dB matched the approximation.\n\n**C3.**\n(a) Step 1: 1 MHz at 20 dB: C = 10⁶ log₂(1 + 100) = 10⁶ × 6.658 = **6.66 Mb/s**.\nStep 2: 3100 Hz at 35 dB (SNR = 3162): C = 3100 × log₂(3163) = 3100 × 11.63 = **36.0 kb/s**.\nStep 3: the Shannon limit E_b/N₀ = ln 2 = **-1.59 dB**.\n(b) d_min = **3**; detects 2 errors and corrects ⌊(3 - 1)/2⌋ = **1** error; all 16 messages × 7 single-error positions = **112 of 112** were corrected (a perfect code).\n(c) Step 1: encode b = 1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0 with c₁ = b ⊕ s₁ ⊕ s₂, c₂ = b ⊕ s₂, state (s₁, s₂) → (b, s₁): outputs **11, 10, 00, 01, 01, 11**.\nStep 2: with the 4th and 9th coded bits flipped, the Viterbi decoder still returned **1 0 1 1 0 0** (path metric 2): the free distance of the code is 5, so any path other than the transmitted one is at least 5 coded bits away.\nStep 3: simulated output BER at p = 0.01 was **5 × 10⁻⁵** (a 200-fold reduction).\n\n**C4.**\n(a) The cyclic prefix copies the last samples of each OFDM symbol in front of it: it absorbs the multipath delay spread (no inter-symbol interference) and turns linear convolution with the channel into circular convolution, so each subcarrier sees a single complex gain H(k) and a one-tap equaliser Y/H restores the data. With 64 subcarriers, a 16-sample prefix, channel h = [1, 0.5, 0.3j, 0.2] and no noise: **0 errors** in 256,000 bits; without the prefix: **487 errors**.\n(b) Step 1: spacing = 20 MHz/64 = **312.5 kHz**.\nStep 2: useful symbol = 64/20 MHz = 3.2 μs; with the 0.8 μs guard interval **4.0 μs**.\nStep 3: rate = 48 × 6 bits × (3/4)/4 μs = 216/4 μs = **54 Mb/s**.\n(c) Step 1: a 7-stage m-sequence has 2⁷ - 1 = **127 chips**.\nStep 2: the periodic autocorrelation is **127 at zero shift and -1 at every other shift**.\nStep 3: processing gain = 10 log₁₀ 127 = **21.0 dB**.\nStep 4: with orthogonal Walsh codes (HHᵀ = 8I) two users sending (1, -1, 1) and (-1, -1, 1) simultaneously are separated perfectly by correlating with their own code (the output for user 1 was exactly 1, -1, 1).\n\n**C5.**\n(a) Step 1: FSPL = 20 log₁₀(d_km) + 20 log₁₀(f_MHz) + 32.44.\nStep 2: GEO satellite, 36,000 km at 4000 MHz: 91.1 + 72.0 + 32.44 = **195.6 dB**.\nStep 3: 1 km at 2400 MHz: 0 + 67.6 + 32.44 = **100.0 dB**.\nStep 4: 20 km at 2000 MHz: 26.0 + 66.0 + 32.44 = **124.5 dB**.\n(b) Step 1: P_r = 20 + 3 + 3 - 100.0 = **-74.0 dBm**.\nStep 2: noise floor = -174 + 10 log₁₀(20 × 10⁶) + 6 = -174 + 73.0 + 6 = **-95.0 dBm**.\nStep 3: SNR = -74.0 - (-95.0) = **21.0 dB**.\n(c) Step 1: 110 = 20 log₁₀(d_km) + 67.6 + 32.44, so 20 log₁₀ d = 9.96.\nStep 2: d = 10^(9.96/20) = **3.15 km** (free space).\n(d) **FDMA**: separate frequency bands (simple, guard bands); **TDMA**: time slots on a common carrier (GSM); **CDMA**: orthogonal or PN codes in the same time and frequency (soft capacity, power control); **OFDMA**: groups of subcarriers allocated in time and frequency (LTE, 5G, Wi-Fi 6: flexible and robust against multipath).","notes":"# Communication Systems: Lecture Notes\n\nA communication system moves information from a source to a destination over a noisy channel. This course covers analog modulation (AM and FM), sampling and quantisation (PCM), digital modulation (BPSK, QPSK, QAM), bit-error performance, channel capacity, error-control coding, OFDM and spread spectrum, and link budgets. All numbers were computed with numpy and scipy, and the bit-error-rate figures are Monte Carlo simulations, so the results shown are real. Prerequisites: Signals & Systems, Probability & Statistics, Digital Signal Processing and Electromagnetics (helpful for the link budget).\n\n# Chapter 1: System Model and Noise\n\nA transmitter (source coding, channel coding, modulation, amplification), a **channel** (cable, fibre, radio) that attenuates, distorts and adds noise, and a receiver (filtering, demodulation, detection, decoding). Performance measures: **signal-to-noise ratio (SNR)**, **bit error rate (BER)**, bandwidth and data rate, power.\n**Thermal noise**: N = k T B with Boltzmann's constant k = 1.38 × 10^-23 J/K. At T = 290 K the noise power density is N0 = kT = 4.0 × 10^-21 W/Hz = **-174 dBm/Hz**; in a 1 MHz bandwidth the noise power is 4.0 × 10^-15 W = **-114 dBm**. Receiver noise figure NF adds to this. **AWGN** (additive white Gaussian noise) is the standard channel model: r = s + n with n Gaussian of variance N0/2 per real dimension.\n**Decibels**: dB = 10 log10(power ratio); dBm is dB relative to 1 mW.\n\n# Chapter 2: Analog Modulation\n\n## 2.1 Amplitude modulation (AM)\nA message m(t) with |m| <= 1 and highest frequency fm modulates a carrier of frequency fc: **s(t) = Ac (1 + m_a cos(2 pi fm t)) cos(2 pi fc t)** for a tone message, with **modulation index m_a** (m_a <= 1 avoids overmodulation and envelope distortion). Spectrum: a carrier of amplitude Ac and two sidebands at fc ± fm of amplitude m_a Ac/2 each. A numerical check (fc = 100 kHz, fm = 5 kHz, m_a = 0.8, Ac = 1): the measured spectral lines were **1.0 at 100 kHz and 0.4 at 95 kHz and 105 kHz** (0.8/2 = 0.4). **Bandwidth = 2 fm** (10 kHz here).\n**Power**: carrier Pc; each sideband Pc m_a^2/4; total P = Pc (1 + m_a^2/2); the **efficiency** (power in the sidebands, which carry the information) is eta = m_a^2/(2 + m_a^2): m_a = 1: **33.3 percent**; m_a = 0.5: **11.1 percent**; m_a = 0.8: 24.2 percent. Example: Pc = 100 W, m_a = 0.8: total **132 W**, of which **32 W** is in the sidebands. Detection: an **envelope detector** (diode, RC) for AM; the RC time constant must satisfy 1/fc << RC << 1/fm.\n**Variants**: **DSB-SC** (carrier suppressed: efficient, needs coherent demodulation, bandwidth 2 fm), **SSB** (one sideband: bandwidth fm, used in HF radio), **VSB** (analog TV). Coherent demodulation multiplies by a local carrier and low-pass filters; a phase error phi reduces the output by cos(phi).\n\n## 2.2 Frequency and phase modulation\nFM: **s(t) = Ac cos(2 pi fc t + beta sin(2 pi fm t))** where the instantaneous frequency is fc + delta_f cos(2 pi fm t) and the **modulation index** is **beta = delta_f/fm** (delta_f is the peak frequency deviation). The spectrum has sidebands at fc ± n fm with amplitudes Ac |J_n(beta)| (Bessel functions). Check: delta_f = 50 kHz, fm = 10 kHz gives beta = 5: measured line amplitudes at fc, fc + fm, fc + 2 fm, fc + 3 fm: **0.1776, 0.3276, 0.0466, 0.3648**, equal to |J0(5)|, |J1(5)|, |J2(5)|, |J3(5)| = 0.1776, 0.3276, 0.0466, 0.3648. At beta = 2.405 the carrier component J0 vanishes (-9 × 10^-5): all power is in the sidebands.\n**Carson's rule**: BW ≈ 2 (delta_f + fm) = 2 fm (beta + 1). Broadcast FM: delta_f = 75 kHz, fm = 15 kHz: beta = **5**, BW = **180 kHz** (channels spaced by 200 kHz). Computed from the Bessel functions: the sidebands within ± 6 fm (6 pairs) contain 98 percent of the power for beta = 5 (and 2, 3 and 11 pairs for beta = 1, 2 and 10), exactly the width Carson's rule gives. FM has **constant envelope** (efficient nonlinear amplifiers), and a **noise-suppression** advantage (SNR improvement proportional to beta^2 above a **threshold**: a bandwidth-for-SNR trade), with **pre-emphasis and de-emphasis** to flatten the noise spectrum. NBFM (beta << 1) has bandwidth about 2 fm.\n\n# Chapter 3: Digitisation (PCM)\n\n**Sampling** at fs >= 2 fm; **quantisation** to 2^n levels; **encoding**. For a full-scale sine wave the signal-to-quantisation-noise ratio is **SQNR = 6.02 n + 1.76 dB**: 8 bits **49.9 dB**, 12 bits **74.0 dB**; to reach 40 dB you need 6.35, i.e. **7 bits**. Non-uniform quantisation (mu-law in North America, A-law in Europe) compresses the dynamic range of speech. Bit rates: telephone speech 8000 samples/s × 8 bits = **64 kb/s**; CD audio 44,100 × 16 × 2 channels = **1.4112 Mb/s**. Delta modulation and ADPCM reduce the rate; lossy codecs (MP3, AAC, Opus) use perceptual models.\n**Line codes** (NRZ, RZ, Manchester, AMI) shape the spectrum, provide timing and DC balance. **Multiplexing**: FDM, TDM (T1: 24 channels × 64 kb/s + framing = 1.544 Mb/s), statistical multiplexing.\n\n# Chapter 4: Digital Modulation and Detection\n\n## 4.1 Signal sets\nBinary phase-shift keying (**BPSK**): bits map to two antipodal waveforms ±sqrt(Eb) g(t). **QPSK**: two bits per symbol on the in-phase and quadrature carriers, with Gray mapping (adjacent points differ in one bit): same bandwidth efficiency as two BPSK channels. **M-ary PSK** and **M-QAM** (quadrature amplitude modulation: 16-QAM carries 4 bits/symbol, 64-QAM 6 bits, 256-QAM 8 bits) increase the bit rate for a given bandwidth but need a higher SNR. **FSK** (frequency-shift keying) is used for simple radios (Bluetooth uses GFSK); **MSK/GMSK** (GSM) have constant envelope. Differential schemes avoid carrier phase recovery.\n**Pulse shaping and ISI**: the Nyquist criterion for zero intersymbol interference requires a pulse that is zero at the other sampling instants; the **raised-cosine** filter with roll-off alpha has bandwidth **(1 + alpha) Rs/2** (baseband) or **(1 + alpha) Rs** for a passband signal at symbol rate Rs. Example: Rs = 1 Msymbol/s, alpha = 0.35: bandwidth **1.35 MHz**; QPSK carries 2 Mb/s, a spectral efficiency of **1.48 b/s/Hz**. Square-root raised-cosine filters at the transmitter and receiver together form the Nyquist pulse (the receive filter is **matched** to the transmit pulse, which maximises the SNR at the sampling instant).\n\n## 4.2 Error performance in AWGN\nWith Eb the energy per bit and N0 the noise density, the optimum (matched-filter) receiver gives for BPSK and for QPSK (per bit, with Gray mapping):\n**BER = Q(sqrt(2 Eb/N0))**, where Q(x) = 0.5 erfc(x/sqrt 2).\nMonte Carlo results (2 million bits per point) agree closely with the theory:\n```\nEb/N0 (dB)   BPSK simulated   QPSK simulated   theory Q(sqrt(2 Eb/N0))\n0            0.0787           -                0.0786\n2            0.0374           -                0.0375\n4            0.01251          0.01256          0.01250\n6            0.00243          0.00241          0.00239\n8            0.000172         0.000212         0.000191\n```\n**QPSK has the same BER per bit as BPSK** but twice the data rate in the same bandwidth. For **16-QAM** with Gray mapping the bit error rate is approximately **0.75 Q(sqrt(0.8 Eb/N0))**: simulated 0.0279 at 6 dB (theory 0.0279), 0.00922 at 8 dB (0.00925) and 0.00177 at 10 dB (0.00175). To get a BER of 10^-3 BPSK needs about 6.8 dB, 16-QAM about 10.5 dB: **higher-order modulation trades SNR for spectral efficiency**.\n\n# Chapter 5: Information Theory and Capacity\n\n**Shannon's channel capacity**: C = B log2(1 + SNR) bits per second for an AWGN channel of bandwidth B: reliable communication is possible at any rate below C (with suitable coding) and impossible above it. Examples: B = 1 MHz, SNR = 20 dB (100): C = **6.66 Mb/s**; a 3 kHz telephone line at 30 dB: 29.9 kb/s. Spectral efficiency of 4 b/s/Hz requires SNR >= 2^4 - 1 = 15 (**11.8 dB**). As the bandwidth tends to infinity C tends to 1.44 P/N0, and the minimum **Eb/N0 = ln 2 = 0.693 = -1.59 dB** (the Shannon limit): no code can work below it. Practical systems with turbo/LDPC codes approach the limit within 1 dB.\n**Entropy** H = -sum p_i log2 p_i bits per symbol is the minimum average bits for lossless source coding (Huffman coding achieves within 1 bit); source coding removes redundancy, channel coding adds structured redundancy.\n\n# Chapter 6: Error-Control Coding\n\n## 6.1 Block codes\nAn (n, k) code maps k data bits to n coded bits; the rate is R = k/n. The **minimum distance d_min** determines the capability: it detects d_min - 1 errors and corrects floor((d_min - 1)/2).\n**Hamming (7,4)**: 4 data bits, 3 parity bits, d_min = **3**: corrects any single-bit error. Generator (systematic): codeword = [d1 d2 d3 d4 p1 p2 p3] with p1 = d1 + d2 + d4, p2 = d1 + d3 + d4, p3 = d2 + d3 + d4 (modulo 2). The data 1011 gives the codeword **1011010**. The parity-check matrix H has columns that are all the non-zero 3-bit patterns, so the **syndrome** s = H r of a received word with one error equals the column of the erroneous position, which identifies it. Verified exhaustively: all 16 messages × 7 single-error positions = **112 of 112** errors were corrected, and the minimum distance computed over all pairs of codewords is 3. For one error at bit 4 (the 4th position) of 1011010 the syndrome is 111. Using Hamming (7,4) with BPSK (coding rate 4/7 reduces the energy per coded bit) the block error rate of 4 data bits is lower than the uncoded rate, even though each coded bit has less energy: block error 3.7 × 10^-2 coded against 4.9 × 10^-2 uncoded at Eb/N0 = 4 dB; 5.4 × 10^-3 against 9.5 × 10^-3 at 6 dB; 2.7 × 10^-4 against 7.6 × 10^-4 at 8 dB.\n**Cyclic codes**: CRC for detection; **BCH and Reed-Solomon** codes correct bursts (used in CDs, QR codes and DVB); **LDPC and turbo codes** approach capacity.\n\n## 6.2 Convolutional codes and Viterbi decoding\nA rate-1/2 convolutional encoder with constraint length K = 3 and generators (7, 5) octal outputs two bits per input bit: c1 = b + s1 + s2, c2 = b + s2 (where s1, s2 are the two previous bits). Encoding 1011 followed by two tail zeros gives **11 10 00 01 01 11** (output stream 1 1 1 0 0 0 0 1 0 1 1 1). The **Viterbi algorithm** finds the maximum-likelihood path through the 4-state trellis by keeping, for each state, the survivor with the smallest accumulated Hamming (hard decision) or Euclidean (soft decision) distance. With two channel bit errors introduced (the 4th and the 9th channel bits) the decoder still returned the correct data 1 0 1 1 0 0 with path metric 2 (the two corrected errors). Simulation over a binary symmetric channel with crossover probability p: **p = 0.01: output BER 5 × 10^-5**; p = 0.03: 1.6 × 10^-3; p = 0.05: 5.9 × 10^-3: the code reduces the error rate by more than a factor of 10 at low p. Soft-decision decoding gains a further 2 dB.\n**ARQ** (retransmission on error detection) and **hybrid ARQ** combine detection with correction; **interleaving** spreads burst errors; **coding gain** is the Eb/N0 saved at a given BER.\n\n# Chapter 7: OFDM and Spread Spectrum\n\n## 7.1 OFDM\n**Orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing** splits the data over N narrowband subcarriers spaced by fs/N, generated by an inverse FFT; a **cyclic prefix** (the last L samples copied to the front) turns the channel's linear convolution into a circular one, so a multipath channel with delay spread shorter than the prefix becomes a set of flat channels, each corrected by a one-tap equaliser Y/H. Simulation: N = 64 subcarriers with QPSK, prefix of 16 samples, a multipath channel h = [1, 0.5, 0.3j, 0.2] and no noise: **0 errors in 256,000 bits** after one-tap equalisation; without the prefix: **487 errors** (inter-symbol and inter-carrier interference).\n**IEEE 802.11a/g example**: 20 MHz sampling, 64-point FFT: subcarrier spacing 20 MHz/64 = **312.5 kHz**; useful symbol 3.2 μs plus 0.8 μs guard interval = **4 μs**; 48 data subcarriers with 64-QAM (6 bits) and code rate 3/4: 48 × 6 × 0.75/4 μs = **54 Mb/s**. OFDM is used in Wi-Fi, LTE/5G, DVB and DSL; its drawbacks are a high peak-to-average power ratio and sensitivity to frequency offset.\n\n## 7.2 Spread spectrum and CDMA\n**Direct-sequence spread spectrum (DSSS)** multiplies each data bit by a fast pseudo-noise (PN) chip sequence, spreading the bandwidth by the **processing gain** Gp = chip rate/bit rate. A maximal-length sequence from a 7-stage shift register has length 2^7 - 1 = **127** chips; its periodic autocorrelation is **127 at zero shift and -1 at all other shifts** (verified), giving a processing gain of 10 log10 127 = **21.0 dB** (interference and jamming are suppressed by this factor after despreading). **CDMA** assigns each user an orthogonal code: the 8 × 8 Walsh-Hadamard codes are mutually orthogonal (checked: H H^T = 8 I). Two users with codes 1 and 2 transmitting data (1, -1, 1) and (-1, -1, 1) at the same time and frequency: correlating the sum with user 1's code recovers **(1, -1, 1)**, with the other user fully rejected. Uses: GPS, 3G cellular, Bluetooth/Zigbee variants (frequency hopping), military anti-jam links.\n\n# Chapter 8: Link Budgets and Multiple Access\n\n**Free-space path loss**: FSPL (dB) = 20 log10(d_km) + 20 log10(f_MHz) + 32.44. A geostationary satellite link (d = 36,000 km, f = 4 GHz): **195.6 dB**; a 1 km Wi-Fi link at 2.4 GHz: **100.0 dB**; 10 km at 900 MHz: 111.5 dB. The **link budget**: P_r = P_t + G_t + G_r - L_path - other losses (all in dB). Example: P_t = 20 dBm, antenna gains 3 dBi each, 1 km at 2.4 GHz: P_r = 20 + 3 + 3 - 100.0 = **-74.0 dBm**; the noise floor for a 20 MHz channel with a 6 dB noise figure is -174 + 10 log10(20 × 10^6) + 6 = **-95.0 dBm**; SNR = **20.9 dB**; fade margin = SNR - required SNR (for example, 64-QAM needs about 22 dB). **Multiple access**: FDMA (frequency slots), TDMA (time slots: GSM), CDMA (codes), OFDMA (subcarrier groups: LTE/5G), CSMA (random access: Wi-Fi). **Cellular concepts**: frequency reuse, handover, cell sizes, and MIMO (multiple antennas multiply the capacity with spatial multiplexing: capacity grows approximately with min(Nt, Nr)).\n\n## Quick reference\n- AM: eta = m^2/(2 + m^2); BW = 2 fm. FM: beta = delta_f/fm; BW ≈ 2 (delta_f + fm).\n- SQNR = 6.02 n + 1.76 dB. BPSK/QPSK BER = Q(sqrt(2 Eb/N0)). Raised-cosine BW = (1 + alpha) Rs.\n- Shannon: C = B log2(1 + SNR); minimum Eb/N0 = -1.59 dB.\n- Hamming (7,4): d_min = 3; corrects 1 error. OFDM spacing = fs/N; the cyclic prefix must exceed the delay spread.\n- FSPL = 20 log10(d_km) + 20 log10(f_MHz) + 32.44 dB; thermal noise = -174 dBm/Hz + 10 log10 B.\n","plan":"# Communication Systems: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 11 hours per week (3 reading, 6 to 7 problems and simulation, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Communications is learned by simulating: generate a signal, add noise, demodulate, count errors, and compare with theory. Use Python (numpy, scipy, matplotlib) or MATLAB/Octave; an inexpensive software-defined radio (RTL-SDR, about 30 dollars) lets you receive real FM, AM aviation and ADS-B signals.\n\n## Textbooks\n- Simon Haykin and Michael Moher, *Communication Systems*. The classic university text for analog and digital communications.\n- John Proakis and Masoud Salehi, *Digital Communications*.\n- Bernard Sklar, *Digital Communications: Fundamentals and Applications*. Practical and example-rich.\n- Andrea Goldsmith, *Wireless Communications*; David Tse and Pramod Viswanath, *Fundamentals of Wireless Communication* (free online).\n- Thomas Cover and Joy Thomas, *Elements of Information Theory*.\n- Richard Lyons, *Understanding Digital Signal Processing* (for the DSP side).\n\n## Free courses and tools\n- MIT OpenCourseWare 6.02 Introduction to EECS II: Digital Communication Systems; Stanford EE 359; Coursera \"Wireless Communications for Everybody\".\n- GNU Radio (graphical SDR toolkit), GNU Octave, the Python `commpy` and `scipy.signal` packages, gqrx and SDR# for the RTL-SDR; Pavan Kumar Vemuri and Dr. Mark Wickert's *Signals and Systems for Dummies* style scripts (scikit-dsp-comm).\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Communication system model, noise, decibels (Ch 1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1. Compute noise floors and practise dB arithmetic.\n\n**Week 2: Amplitude modulation (Ch 2.1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q2, Q3, Q7(a). Generate and demodulate AM in Python; look at the spectrum.\n\n**Week 3: Frequency modulation (Ch 2.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q4, Q5, Q7(b, c). Verify the Bessel-function line amplitudes.\n\n**Week 4: Sampling, quantisation, PCM (Ch 3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q6. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week (Chapters 1 to 4.1). Quantise an audio file to different bit depths and listen.\n\n**Week 5: Digital modulation: BPSK, QPSK, QAM (Ch 4.1).** Do: Assignment 2 Q7. Plot constellations with noise.\n\n**Week 6: Detection and BER in AWGN (Ch 4.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1 to Q3. Simulate BPSK, QPSK and 16-QAM and compare with theory.\n\n**Week 7: Pulse shaping, ISI, Nyquist criteria (Ch 4.1).** Do: Assignment 2 Q4, Q6. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam. Draw an eye diagram with raised-cosine pulses.\n\n**Week 8: Information theory and Shannon capacity (Ch 5).** Do: Assignment 2 Q5. Compute the capacity of real channels (ADSL, Wi-Fi).\n\n**Week 9: Block codes (Ch 6.1).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1, Q2. Implement Hamming (7,4) encoding and syndrome decoding.\n\n**Week 10: Convolutional codes and the Viterbi algorithm (Ch 6.2).** Do: Assignment 3 Q3. Implement the encoder and decoder and plot BER versus p.\n\n**Week 11: OFDM (Ch 7.1).** Do: Assignment 3 Q4. Simulate OFDM through a multipath channel with and without the cyclic prefix.\n\n**Week 12: Spread spectrum, CDMA, multiple access, link budgets (Ch 7.2, 8).** Do: Assignment 3 Q5 to Q7. Compute the link budget of a satellite or Wi-Fi link.\n\n**Week 13: Project.** Build and simulate an end-to-end digital link (source, coder, modulator, channel, receiver) in Python or GNU Radio, or receive a real signal with an SDR (FM broadcast, ADS-B, weather satellite images); report BER curves or decoded results.\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo missed Class Test and assignment questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, formula sheet only).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can compute noise power, SNR and link budgets in decibels.\n- [ ] I can analyse AM and FM signals: spectra, bandwidth, power and modulation index.\n- [ ] I can compute PCM bit rates and SQNR.\n- [ ] I can compute the BER of BPSK, QPSK and QAM in AWGN and explain Gray coding, matched filtering and the Nyquist criterion.\n- [ ] I can compute channel capacity and relate spectral efficiency to required SNR.\n- [ ] I can encode and decode Hamming codes and explain convolutional coding and Viterbi decoding.\n- [ ] I can explain OFDM, the cyclic prefix and the 802.11 numbers.\n- [ ] I can explain spread spectrum, processing gain and CDMA.\n- [ ] I can compare multiple-access methods and explain cellular reuse.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with a formula sheet only.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. Always simulate: a Monte Carlo BER curve next to the theoretical curve catches algebra mistakes.\n2. Keep units straight: dB, dBm, linear ratios; Eb/N0 versus SNR; bit versus symbol.\n3. Sketch spectra for every modulation: where are the carrier, sidebands, and the occupied bandwidth?\n4. Use the bandwidth/power/complexity trade-off as the lens for every scheme.\n5. Listen to and look at real signals with an SDR: it makes the theory concrete.\n","test":"# Communication Systems: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each).\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** The thermal noise power density at 290 K is about\nA) -204 dBm/Hz  B) -174 dBm/Hz  C) -144 dBm/Hz  D) -114 dBm/Hz\n\n**2.** The bandwidth of a conventional AM signal with message bandwidth fm is\nA) fm  B) 2 fm  C) 4 fm  D) fm/2\n\n**3.** The power efficiency of AM with modulation index m = 1 is\nA) 100 percent  B) 66.7 percent  C) 33.3 percent  D) 11.1 percent\n\n**4.** Overmodulation of AM occurs when\nA) m > 1  B) m < 1  C) m = 0  D) fc < fm\n\n**5.** The spectrum of a single-tone AM wave has a carrier and\nA) one sideband  B) two sidebands of amplitude m Ac/2 each  C) infinitely many sidebands  D) no sidebands\n\n**6.** The modulation index of FM is\nA) delta_f × fm  B) delta_f / fm  C) fm / delta_f  D) fc / fm\n\n**7.** Carson's rule gives the FM bandwidth as\nA) 2 (delta_f + fm)  B) delta_f  C) 2 fm  D) fm\n\n**8.** Compared with AM, wideband FM\nA) needs less bandwidth  B) has better noise performance at the cost of bandwidth  C) has no constant envelope  D) is simpler to demodulate\n\n**9.** The SQNR of an n-bit quantiser for a full-scale sine wave is about\nA) 3 n dB  B) 6.02 n + 1.76 dB  C) 20 n dB  D) n^2 dB\n\n**10.** Telephone speech sampled at 8 kHz with 8 bits has a bit rate of\nA) 8 kb/s  B) 16 kb/s  C) 64 kb/s  D) 128 kb/s\n\n**11.** The bit error rate of BPSK in AWGN is\nA) Q(sqrt(Eb/N0))  B) Q(sqrt(2 Eb/N0))  C) exp(-Eb/N0)  D) 1/(1 + Eb/N0)\n\n**12.** QPSK compared with BPSK in the same bandwidth gives\nA) twice the bit rate at the same BER per bit  B) the same bit rate  C) half the BER  D) half the bandwidth\n\n**13.** Gray coding of constellation points ensures that\nA) neighbouring points differ in one bit  B) all points have equal energy  C) the bandwidth is minimal  D) ISI is zero\n\n**14.** The raised-cosine pulse with roll-off alpha and symbol rate Rs needs a passband bandwidth of\nA) Rs  B) alpha Rs  C) (1 + alpha) Rs  D) 2 Rs\n\n**15.** The Shannon capacity of an AWGN channel is\nA) B SNR  B) B log2(1 + SNR)  C) B log10 SNR  D) SNR / B\n\n**16.** The Shannon limit for Eb/N0 is\nA) 0 dB  B) -1.59 dB  C) 3 dB  D) 10 dB\n\n**17.** The minimum distance of the Hamming (7,4) code is\nA) 1  B) 2  C) 3  D) 4\n\n**18.** A code with minimum distance d_min = 5 can correct\nA) 1 error  B) 2 errors  C) 4 errors  D) 5 errors\n\n**19.** In OFDM the cyclic prefix is used to\nA) increase the data rate  B) remove noise  C) absorb multipath delay spread and keep subcarriers orthogonal  D) reduce power\n\n**20.** The processing gain of a direct-sequence system with a 127-chip code is about\nA) 7 dB  B) 12 dB  C) 21 dB  D) 127 dB\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. AM and FM (10 marks)** (a) A transmitter has Pc = 200 W and m = 0.5: the total power, the sideband power and the efficiency [4] (b) FM with delta_f = 30 kHz and fm = 5 kHz: beta and Carson's bandwidth [3] (c) The AM bandwidth for fm = 5 kHz [1] (d) The SQNR for 10 bits [2]\n\n**B2. Digital modulation (10 marks)** (a) The Eb/N0 in dB needed for BPSK BER = 10^-3 [3] (b) Why QPSK has the same BER as BPSK per bit [3] (c) QPSK at Rs = 2 Msymbol/s with raised-cosine alpha = 0.25: the bit rate, bandwidth and spectral efficiency [4]\n\n**B3. Capacity and coding (10 marks)** (a) The capacity of a channel with B = 4 MHz and SNR = 15 dB [3] (b) The SNR needed for 4 b/s/Hz [2] (c) Decode the Hamming (7,4) word 1011110 (parity p1 = d1 + d2 + d4, p2 = d1 + d3 + d4, p3 = d2 + d3 + d4; word [d1 d2 d3 d4 p1 p2 p3]) [5]\n\n**B4. OFDM, spread spectrum and link budget (10 marks)** (a) An OFDM system with 256 subcarriers and fs = 20 MHz: the subcarrier spacing and symbol duration [3] (b) A 5-stage m-sequence: the length and the processing gain [3] (c) A 5.8 GHz link of 100 m, Pt = 10 dBm, antenna gains 3 dBi each: the free-space path loss and the received power [4]\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **B** · 2 **B** · 3 **C** · 4 **A** · 5 **B** · 6 **B** · 7 **A** · 8 **B** · 9 **B** · 10 **C** · 11 **B** · 12 **A** · 13 **A** · 14 **C** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **C** · 18 **B** · 19 **C** · 20 **C**\n\nBrief reasons:\n1. Step 1: N₀ = kT = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 290 = 4.0 × 10⁻²¹ W/Hz. Step 2: in dBm/Hz: 10 log₁₀(4.0 × 10⁻¹⁸) = -174 dBm/Hz.\n2. AM has two sidebands, each of width f_m, around the carrier: bandwidth 2f_m.\n3. Step 1: efficiency η = m²/(2 + m²). Step 2: m = 1: 1/3 = 33.3 percent.\n4. Overmodulation occurs when m > 1: the envelope would go negative and the wave is distorted.\n5. A single-tone AM wave contains the carrier plus two sidebands at f_c ± f_m, each of amplitude mA_c/2.\n6. FM modulation index β = Δf/f_m (peak deviation divided by message frequency).\n7. Carson's rule: B ≈ 2(Δf + f_m).\n8. Wideband FM trades bandwidth for noise performance (the FM improvement), and has a constant envelope.\n9. SQNR of an n-bit quantiser for a full-scale sine wave: 6.02n + 1.76 dB.\n10. Step 1: bit rate = sampling rate × bits per sample. Step 2: 8000 × 8 = 64,000 b/s = 64 kb/s.\n11. BPSK in AWGN: BER = Q(√(2E_b/N₀)).\n12. QPSK sends 2 bits per symbol on orthogonal carriers, giving twice the bit rate of BPSK in the same bandwidth at the same BER per bit.\n13. Gray coding makes neighbouring constellation points differ in exactly one bit.\n14. Raised-cosine pulse bandwidth: (1 + α)R_s.\n15. Shannon capacity: C = B log₂(1 + SNR).\n16. The Shannon limit on E_b/N₀ as bandwidth → ∞ is ln 2 = 0.693, i.e. -1.59 dB.\n17. The Hamming (7,4) code has minimum distance d_min = 3.\n18. Step 1: correctable errors t = ⌊(d_min - 1)/2⌋. Step 2: ⌊(5 - 1)/2⌋ = 2.\n19. The cyclic prefix absorbs the multipath delay spread and keeps the subcarriers orthogonal.\n20. Step 1: processing gain = 10 log₁₀(chips). Step 2: 10 log₁₀ 127 = 21 dB.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) Step 1: P_t = P_c(1 + m²/2) = 200(1 + 0.25/2) = 200 × 1.125 = **225 W**.\nStep 2: sideband power = 225 - 200 = **25 W**.\nStep 3: efficiency = 25/225 = **11.1 percent** (= m²/(2 + m²) = 0.25/2.25).\n(b) Step 1: β = Δf/f_m = 30/5 = **6**.\nStep 2: Carson bandwidth = 2(Δf + f_m) = 2(30 + 5) = **70 kHz**.\n(c) AM bandwidth = 2f_m = 2 × 5 = **10 kHz**.\n(d) SQNR = 6.02n + 1.76 = 6.02(10) + 1.76 = 60.2 + 1.76 = **62.0 dB**.\n\n**B2.**\n(a) Step 1: BER = Q(√(2E_b/N₀)) = 10⁻³ requires √(2E_b/N₀) = 3.09.\nStep 2: E_b/N₀ = 3.09²/2 = 9.55/2 = 4.77; in dB: 10 log₁₀ 4.77 = **6.79 dB**.\n(b) QPSK sends two bits on orthogonal (in-phase and quadrature) carriers. With Gray mapping each bit is detected independently with the same distance-to-noise ratio as BPSK (energy per bit E_b and independent noise in each quadrature), so BER = Q(√(2E_b/N₀)). The symbol rate is half the bit rate, so for the same bit rate QPSK needs half the bandwidth.\n(c) Step 1: bit rate = 2R_s = 2 × 2 = **4 Mb/s**.\nStep 2: bandwidth = (1 + α)R_s = 1.25 × 2 = **2.5 MHz**.\nStep 3: spectral efficiency = 4/2.5 = **1.6 b/s/Hz**.\n\n**B3.**\n(a) Step 1: SNR = 10^(15/10) = 10^1.5 = 31.62.\nStep 2: C = B log₂(1 + SNR) = 4 × 10⁶ × log₂(32.62) = 4 × 10⁶ × 5.028.\n**C = 20.1 Mb/s**.\n(b) Step 1: 4 b/s/Hz = log₂(1 + SNR), so 1 + SNR = 2⁴ = 16 and SNR = 15.\nStep 2: in dB: 10 log₁₀ 15 = **11.76 dB**.\n(c) Received word r = 1011110: d₁d₂d₃d₄ = 1011 and p₁p₂p₃ = 110.\nStep 1: s₁ = d₁ + d₂ + d₄ + p₁ = 1 + 0 + 1 + 1 = 3 → 1.\nStep 2: s₂ = d₁ + d₃ + d₄ + p₂ = 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4 → 0.\nStep 3: s₃ = d₂ + d₃ + d₄ + p₃ = 0 + 1 + 1 + 0 = 2 → 0.\nStep 4: syndrome [1, 0, 0], the column belonging to parity bit p₁ (the 5th bit), the only bit that appears in check 1 alone.\nStep 5: flip bit 5: 1011010, so the data are **1011** (the error was in a parity bit).\n\n**B4.**\n(a) Step 1: subcarrier spacing = f_s/N = 20 MHz/256 = **78.125 kHz**.\nStep 2: useful symbol duration = N/f_s = 256/(20 × 10⁶) = **12.8 μs** (plus the guard interval).\n(b) Step 1: length of a 5-stage m-sequence = 2⁵ - 1 = **31** chips.\nStep 2: processing gain = 10 log₁₀ 31 = **14.9 dB**.\n(c) Step 1: FSPL = 20 log₁₀(d_km) + 20 log₁₀(f_MHz) + 32.44 with d = 0.1 km, f = 5800 MHz: -20 + 75.27 + 32.44 = **87.7 dB**.\nStep 2: received power = 10 dBm + 3 dBi + 3 dBi - 87.7 dB = **-71.7 dBm**.\n(With a noise floor of about -91 dBm in 40 MHz and a 7 dB noise figure, the SNR is 19.3 dB.)"},mcq:[{"q":"The thermal noise power density at 290 K is about","options":["-204 dBm/Hz","-174 dBm/Hz","-144 dBm/Hz","-114 dBm/Hz"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1: N₀ = kT = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 290 = 4.0 × 10⁻²¹ W/Hz. Step 2: in dBm/Hz: 10 log₁₀(4.0 × 10⁻¹⁸) = -174 dBm/Hz.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The bandwidth of a conventional AM signal with message bandwidth fm is","options":["fm","2 fm","4 fm","fm/2"],"answer":1,"explain":"AM has two sidebands, each of width f_m, around the carrier: bandwidth 2f_m.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The power efficiency of AM with modulation index m = 1 is","options":["100 percent","66.7 percent","33.3 percent","11.1 percent"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: efficiency η = m²/(2 + m²). Step 2: m = 1: 1/3 = 33.3 percent.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Overmodulation of AM occurs when","options":["m > 1","m < 1","m = 0","fc < fm"],"answer":0,"explain":"Overmodulation occurs when m > 1: the envelope would go negative and the wave is distorted.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The spectrum of a single-tone AM wave has a carrier and","options":["one sideband","two sidebands of amplitude m Ac/2 each","infinitely many sidebands","no sidebands"],"answer":1,"explain":"A single-tone AM wave contains the carrier plus two sidebands at f_c ± f_m, each of amplitude mA_c/2.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The modulation index of FM is","options":["delta_f × fm","delta_f / fm","fm / delta_f","fc / fm"],"answer":1,"explain":"FM modulation index β = Δf/f_m (peak deviation divided by message frequency).","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Carson's rule gives the FM bandwidth as","options":["2 (delta_f + fm)","delta_f","2 fm","fm"],"answer":0,"explain":"Carson's rule: B ≈ 2(Δf + f_m).","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Compared with AM, wideband FM","options":["needs less bandwidth","has better noise performance at the cost of bandwidth","has no constant envelope","is simpler to demodulate"],"answer":1,"explain":"Wideband FM trades bandwidth for noise performance (the FM improvement), and has a constant envelope.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The SQNR of an n-bit quantiser for a full-scale sine wave is about","options":["3 n dB","6.02 n + 1.76 dB","20 n dB","n^2 dB"],"answer":1,"explain":"SQNR of an n-bit quantiser for a full-scale sine wave: 6.02n + 1.76 dB.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Telephone speech sampled at 8 kHz with 8 bits has a bit rate of","options":["8 kb/s","16 kb/s","64 kb/s","128 kb/s"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: bit rate = sampling rate × bits per sample. Step 2: 8000 × 8 = 64,000 b/s = 64 kb/s.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The bit error rate of BPSK in AWGN is","options":["Q(sqrt(Eb/N0))","Q(sqrt(2 Eb/N0))","exp(-Eb/N0)","1/(1 + Eb/N0)"],"answer":1,"explain":"BPSK in AWGN: BER = Q(√(2E_b/N₀)).","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"QPSK compared with BPSK in the same bandwidth gives","options":["twice the bit rate at the same BER per bit","the same bit rate","half the BER","half the bandwidth"],"answer":0,"explain":"QPSK sends 2 bits per symbol on orthogonal carriers, giving twice the bit rate of BPSK in the same bandwidth at the same BER per bit.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Gray coding of constellation points ensures that","options":["neighbouring points differ in one bit","all points have equal energy","the bandwidth is minimal","ISI is zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"Gray coding makes neighbouring constellation points differ in exactly one bit.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The raised-cosine pulse with roll-off alpha and symbol rate Rs needs a passband bandwidth of","options":["Rs","alpha Rs","(1 + alpha) Rs","2 Rs"],"answer":2,"explain":"Raised-cosine pulse bandwidth: (1 + α)R_s.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The Shannon capacity of an AWGN channel is","options":["B SNR","B log2(1 + SNR)","B log10 SNR","SNR / B"],"answer":1,"explain":"Shannon capacity: C = B log₂(1 + SNR).","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The Shannon limit for Eb/N0 is","options":["0 dB","-1.59 dB","3 dB","10 dB"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Shannon limit on E_b/N₀ as bandwidth → ∞ is ln 2 = 0.693, i.e. -1.59 dB.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The minimum distance of the Hamming (7,4) code is","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Hamming (7,4) code has minimum distance d_min = 3.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A code with minimum distance d_min = 5 can correct","options":["1 error","2 errors","4 errors","5 errors"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1: correctable errors t = ⌊(d_min - 1)/2⌋. Step 2: ⌊(5 - 1)/2⌋ = 2.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In OFDM the cyclic prefix is used to","options":["increase the data rate","remove noise","absorb multipath delay spread and keep subcarriers orthogonal","reduce power"],"answer":2,"explain":"The cyclic prefix absorbs the multipath delay spread and keeps the subcarriers orthogonal.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The processing gain of a direct-sequence system with a 127-chip code is about","options":["7 dB","12 dB","21 dB","127 dB"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: processing gain = 10 log₁₀(chips). Step 2: 10 log₁₀ 127 = 21 dB.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The noise power in a 1 MHz bandwidth at 290 K is about","options":["-174 dBm","-144 dBm","-114 dBm","-84 dBm"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: N = kTB; in dBm: -174 + 10 log₁₀ B(Hz). Step 2: 10 log₁₀(10⁶) = 60, so N = -174 + 60 = -114 dBm.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In AM, the sideband power for carrier power Pc and modulation index m is","options":["Pc m^2/2","Pc m","Pc/m","2 Pc"],"answer":0,"explain":"Step 1: each sideband carries P_c m²/4. Step 2: the two sidebands together carry P_c m²/2.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The envelope detector requires a time constant RC such that","options":["RC >> 1/fm","RC << 1/fc","RC = 1/fc","1/fc << RC << 1/fm"],"answer":3,"explain":"Step 1: the capacitor must follow the envelope but not the carrier. Step 2: so 1/f_c ≪ RC ≪ 1/f_m.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which AM variant has the narrowest bandwidth?","options":["DSB-SC","conventional AM","SSB","FM"],"answer":2,"explain":"SSB transmits only one sideband, so its bandwidth is f_m, the narrowest of the AM variants (DSB-SC and AM need 2f_m).","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"An FM signal with delta_f = 75 kHz and fm = 15 kHz has a modulation index of","options":["0.2","5","15","75"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1: β = Δf/f_m. Step 2: = 75/15 = 5.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The carrier component of a single-tone FM wave is proportional to","options":["J1(beta)","beta","J0(beta)","1/beta"],"answer":2,"explain":"The carrier component of a single-tone FM wave is proportional to J₀(β).","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The SQNR of an ideal 12-bit quantiser is about","options":["49.9 dB","62 dB","74 dB","98 dB"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: SQNR = 6.02n + 1.76. Step 2: = 6.02 × 12 + 1.76 = 72.24 + 1.76 = 74.0 dB.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Nyquist criterion for zero ISI requires a pulse that","options":["has zero value at the other sampling instants","is rectangular","has infinite energy","is periodic"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Nyquist criterion for zero ISI requires a pulse that is zero at all other sampling instants.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The BER of 16-QAM at a given Eb/N0 is","options":["lower than QPSK","the same as QPSK","higher than QPSK","zero"],"answer":2,"explain":"At the same E_b/N₀ 16-QAM has points closer together, so its BER is higher than QPSK.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A matched filter","options":["maximises the SNR at the sampling instant","removes all noise","is a high-pass filter","has a flat response"],"answer":0,"explain":"A matched filter maximises the SNR at the sampling instant.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Gray coding gives","options":["the minimum bandwidth","one bit difference between adjacent constellation points","the maximum power","zero ISI"],"answer":1,"explain":"Gray coding makes adjacent constellation points differ in one bit.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For a channel with SNR = 1 (0 dB) the capacity per hertz is","options":["0 b/s/Hz","0.5 b/s/Hz","1 b/s/Hz","2 b/s/Hz"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: C/B = log₂(1 + SNR). Step 2: SNR = 1: log₂ 2 = 1 b/s/Hz.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The minimum Eb/N0 for reliable communication is","options":["-1.59 dB","0 dB","3 dB","6 dB"],"answer":0,"explain":"The minimum E_b/N₀ for reliable communication (Shannon limit) is ln 2 = 0.693, that is -1.59 dB.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A code with minimum distance 3 can","options":["correct 1 error","correct 2 errors","detect 3 errors","correct 3 errors"],"answer":0,"explain":"Step 1: correctable errors t = ⌊(d_min - 1)/2⌋. Step 2: ⌊(3 - 1)/2⌋ = 1; the code can detect 2.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The syndrome of a received Hamming word identifies","options":["the data","the position of a single error","the CRC","the carrier"],"answer":1,"explain":"The syndrome identifies the position of a single-bit error.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Viterbi algorithm finds","options":["the shortest path in a network","the maximum-likelihood path through the trellis","the generator polynomial","the interleaver"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Viterbi algorithm finds the maximum-likelihood path through the trellis.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The cyclic prefix in OFDM must be at least as long as","options":["the symbol","the sampling period","the delay spread of the channel","the carrier period"],"answer":2,"explain":"The cyclic prefix must be at least as long as the delay spread of the channel.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A 63-chip PN sequence gives a processing gain of about","options":["6 dB","12 dB","18 dB","63 dB"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: processing gain = 10 log₁₀(chips). Step 2: 10 log₁₀ 63 = 18.0 dB.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Free-space path loss increases by about how much when the distance is doubled?","options":["3 dB","6 dB","12 dB","20 dB"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1: FSPL ∝ 20 log₁₀ d. Step 2: doubling the distance adds 20 log₁₀ 2 = 6.02 dB.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"OFDMA is the multiple-access scheme of","options":["AMPS","GSM","IS-95","LTE and 5G"],"answer":3,"explain":"OFDMA is the multiple-access scheme of LTE and 5G.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
